+# Answer: `fs.rmSync` options in Node 22
+
+**Question:** We call `fs.rmSync(dir)` to delete a folder and it throws on non-empty folders. What options does Node 22 document for `fs.rmSync`, and which one fixes this?
+
+## Short answer
+
+Use **`recursive: true`**:
+
+```js
+fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true });               // fixes the error
+fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });  // also no error if dir is already gone (like `rm -rf`)
+```
+
+## Documented options (Node 22)
+
+Source: https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/fs.html#fsrmsyncpath-options (Node v22.x docs, read 2026-11-11). Signature: `fs.rmSync(path[, options])`, added in v14.14.0. It "Synchronously removes files and directories (modeled on the standard POSIX `rm` utility). Returns `undefined`."
+
+| Option | Type | Default | Documented meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `force` | boolean | `false` | When `true`, exceptions are ignored if `path` does not exist. |
+| `maxRetries` | integer | `0` | Number of retries when `EBUSY`, `EMFILE`, `ENFILE`, `ENOTEMPTY` or `EPERM` is hit, with linear backoff of `retryDelay` ms. **Ignored unless `recursive` is `true`.** |
+| `recursive` | boolean | `false` | If `true`, perform a recursive directory removal. In recursive mode, failed operations are retried. |
+| `retryDelay` | integer | `100` | Milliseconds to wait between retries. **Ignored unless `recursive` is `true`.** |
+
+`path` may be a `string`, `Buffer`, or `URL` (`file:` URL support added in v17.3.0 / v16.14.0).
+
+## Which one fixes it, and why
+
+- **`recursive: true`** is the fix *(read from the docs; confirmed by test)*. Without it, `rmSync` won't remove a directory.
+- **`force` does not fix it** *(read from the docs)*. It only stops the error when the path doesn't exist.
+- `maxRetries` and `retryDelay` only apply when `recursive` is on *(read from the docs)*. They help with transient errors such as `EBUSY` on Windows, not with this one.
+
+## Test run (Node v22.23.4, on this machine)
+
+| Call | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `fs.rmSync(nonEmptyDir)` | throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR` ("Path is a directory: rm returned EISDIR") |
+| `fs.rmSync(emptyDir)` | **also** throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR` |
+| `fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true })` | removes the directory and its contents |
+| `fs.rmSync(missingDir)` | throws `ENOENT` |
+| `fs.rmSync(missingDir, { force: true })` | no error |
+
+Note: the error isn't really about the folder being "non-empty". Without `recursive`, `fs.rmSync` refuses to remove *any* directory, even an empty one. To remove only empty directories, use `fs.rmdirSync(dir)` instead *(inferred; not tested here)*.
+
+## Open questions
+
+- None for the main question. I didn't check behaviour on other Node 22 patch releases or on Windows; the docs above cover the whole v22.x line.
